[38;5;231m⏺[39m Then the scope needs flagging before I ask anything detailed: [1mnotifications presuppose an app that doesn't exist yet.[0m
  "Users get notified when tasks they care about change" needs tasks (none), a notion of a user (none), a relationship
  that makes a task one you "care about" (none), and something that counts as a "change" (nothing changes yet). I'd be
  inventing the task data model as a side effect of a notifications spec, in the wrong document.

  So this decomposes into sub-projects, and the shape of that decomposition hinges on one thing.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "users" means several real people, or just you.

  - [1mMulti-user[0m — tasks are shared or assigned, so "tasks they care about" is a real subscription relation between
    people. This requires a server, accounts, auth, and sync before a single notification can fire. Three sub-projects
    minimum (tasks → identity/sync → notifications), and the notification design depends on the first two. Expensive,
    and very hard to retrofit away later.
  - [1mSingle-user[0m — "notify me" means reminders and a change log for one person in one browser. No server, no accounts.
    Tasks plus notifications plausibly fit one modest spec. Cheap now, and adding real multi-user later means redoing
    the storage and identity layers — a genuine rewrite, not an extension.

  This is the expensive-to-reverse direction, which is why it's the question and not an assumption I make quietly.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m single-user, unless you actually have other people in mind. Most of the value ("what changed since
  I looked?", "this is due soon") lands without a server, and it's the version we can design honestly against a task
  model we control. If you do need multi-user, I'd want to build tasks first and spec notifications after — not guess at
  both at once.
